menu "osynth Synthesizer"

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_BLE
        bool "Enable BLE control (mobile app)"
        default y
        help
            Custom GATT service with a binary TLV protocol for the companion
            mobile app. See docs/BLE_PROTOCOL.md.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        bool "Enable I2S output to an external DAC"
        default n
        help
            Routes audio to an external I2S DAC (e.g. PCM5102) for higher
            quality output. Pinout in docs/HARDWARE.md. Off by default.

    choice OSYNTH_I2S_FRONTEND
        prompt "I2S analogue front end"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default OSYNTH_FRONTEND_DISCRETE
        help
            What is on the other end of the I2S port. This picks the
            *analogue* hardware; the digital side (block format, sample rate,
            the render chain) is identical either way.

        config OSYNTH_FRONTEND_DISCRETE
            bool "Discrete: PCM5102A DAC, optional PCM1808 ADC"
            help
                Separate converter chips, each strapped by pins and needing
                no firmware at all. The best-sounding option — a PCM5102A is
                a ~112 dB DAC against the ES8388's ~96 — and the one to pick
                if the line output is what the instrument is judged on.

                Line input needs a second chip (PCM1808) and two more GPIOs.

        config OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
            bool "ES8388 codec (line in + line out on one chip)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
            help
                One chip for both directions, as found on the ESP32-A1S,
                LyraT and most "ESP32 audio kit" boards. Converter quality is
                a step below a discrete PCM5102A, but it integrates the ADC,
                a microphone preamp and a headphone driver, and it is what is
                already soldered to those boards.

                Unlike the discrete chips the ES8388 has no strapping pins
                worth the name: it comes up unconfigured and silent, and
                stays that way until the firmware writes its registers over
                I2C. That is what OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_* below is for. It also
                needs MCLK in slave mode, so enabling this turns the I2S port
                into the same 32-bit-slot / 64x fs / MCLK arrangement the
                line input uses, whether or not capture is enabled.

                The analogue input and output pin pairs are selectable below,
                because boards wire them differently.
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_USB_AUDIO_TAP
        bool "Also stream to USB while the I2S DAC plays"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC && (IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4)
        default y
        help
            Sends every rendered block to the external I2S DAC and the USB
            audio interface at once, instead of the I2S output replacing the
            USB one.

            The DAC keeps the clock: its DMA still paces the render loop, and
            the USB write is best-effort, so a host that stalls mid-stream
            cannot stall the audio task. The two clocks are independent and
            drift apart; the USB class driver absorbs that by varying the
            isochronous packet between 47, 48 and 49 frames, which costs no
            samples and no resampling. Its correction range is roughly
            +/-1875 ppm, against the tens of ppm two crystals actually
            differ by.

            Costs a 256-byte copy per block - well under 1% of the block
            budget, and the USB stack is already running for MIDI either way
            - plus the EP-IN FIFO (OSYNTH_USB_FIFO_MS in usb_descriptors.h).

            Turning this off restores the older either/or behaviour, where
            enabling the I2S DAC leaves the USB audio interface enumerated
            but silent.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        bool "Stereo line input (I2S capture on the DAC's port)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC && (IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4)
        default y if OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default n
        help
            Adds a stereo line input on the *same* I2S port as the output —
            a PCM1808 alongside the PCM5102A on a discrete front end, or the
            ADC half of the ES8388 on a codec one. The I2S controller on the
            S3 and P4 is full duplex: a TX and an RX channel allocated
            together share BCLK and WS, so the converter runs as a slave off
            the two clock lines the output already drives and the capture is
            sample-locked to the playback by construction. Nothing drifts and
            nothing is ever resampled.

            The cost on the wire is that BCLK doubles, from 32x to 64x the
            sample rate. The PCM1808 always emits 24 bits inside a 32-bit
            slot and cannot be told otherwise, so the port has to run 32-bit
            slots; the firmware still reads and writes 16-bit samples, taken
            from the top of each slot. (The ES8388 is told the word length
            over I2C and is happy either way, but runs the same arrangement
            so there is one configuration to reason about.) MCLK becomes a
            real pin for the same reason - both converters need a system
            clock (12.288 MHz = 256x fs) even in slave mode, where the
            PCM5102A never did.

            On an ES8388 build this defaults on, since the ADC is already in
            the chip and costs no extra hardware. Turning it off leaves the
            codec playing and powers its ADC down.

            `in.route` places the input in the render chain, and what it
            records follows from where it lands:

              off  silent
              mon  monitored dry, mixed after the looper's record tap, so it
                   is heard but never printed into a take
              fx   into the FX bus, so it is reverberated *and* recorded
              dry  after the FX bus, recorded without them

            `in.gain` (0..4) is a digital trim after the ADC; clipping in
            front of the ADC is analogue and no firmware gain undoes it,
            which is why the `in pk` meter in the heartbeat reads *before*
            this gain.

            Monitoring latency is one DMA buffer plus a block, about 8-9 ms.
            The whole feature costs roughly 0.7% of the block budget and 256
            bytes of buffer. Off by default, and a build with it off is
            byte-for-byte the output path that shipped before it existed.

            Not on the classic ESP32: there the MCLK output cannot go through
            the GPIO matrix (it is routed via esp_clock_output, which is
            restricted to the CLK_OUT/strapping pins), and that chip's I2S
            treats a duplex configuration mismatch as a hard error.

            Wiring, straps and levels: docs/HARDWARE.md.

    # All five I2S pins are configurable, not just the two the line input
    # added: ES8388 boards come with their pinout already soldered, and a
    # hardcoded BCLK/WS/DOUT triple would rule most of them out.
    config OSYNTH_I2S_BCLK_GPIO
        int "I2S BCLK GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default 27 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 17 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 16

    config OSYNTH_I2S_WS_GPIO
        int "I2S LRCK/WS GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default 32 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 18 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 17

    config OSYNTH_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
        int "I2S DOUT GPIO (ESP32 -> DAC data in)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default 33 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 19 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 18
        help
            PCM5102A DIN, or ES8388 DSDIN.

    config OSYNTH_I2S_DIN_GPIO
        int "I2S DIN GPIO (ADC data out -> ESP32)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        default 20 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 15
        help
            PCM1808 DOUT, or ES8388 ASDOUT. On the S3, keep clear of
            GPIO33-37, which are the octal PSRAM the looper's buffers live in.

    config OSYNTH_I2S_MCLK_GPIO
        int "I2S MCLK GPIO (system clock -> converter)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN || OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default 16 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 14
        help
            Drives the converter's system clock at 256x the sample rate
            (12.288 MHz at 48 kHz) — PCM1808 SCKI, or ES8388 MCLK.
            Mandatory for both: neither is a PCM5102A, and neither can
            reconstruct a clock from BCLK alone. Without it a PCM1808 reads
            silence while the starve counter climbs, and an ES8388 does not
            play at all.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_SDA_GPIO
        int "ES8388 I2C SDA GPIO (codec CDATA pin)"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default 7 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 8
        help
            Goes to the codec's CDATA pin (its control pins are named CCLK
            and CDATA, not SCL/SDA).

            The firmware enables the ESP32's internal pull-ups so a bare
            board has some chance of working, but they are around 45k and
            the ES8388 datasheet asks for **1k** pull-ups on both lines —
            roughly 45x stronger. Fit real 1k resistors to 3V3. Ready-made
            codec boards already have them.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_SCL_GPIO
        int "ES8388 I2C SCL GPIO (codec CCLK pin)"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default 8 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 9
        help
            Goes to the codec's CCLK pin. Same 1k pull-up as CDATA above;
            the datasheet also suggests an R-C low-pass on this line.

    choice OSYNTH_ES8388_INPUT
        prompt "ES8388 analogue input"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388 && OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        default OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE2
        help
            Which of the codec's analogue input pairs the ADC listens to.
            Boards differ, and there is no way to detect it: on the ESP32-A1S
            and LyraT, LIN1/RIN1 is the on-board microphone and LIN2/RIN2 is
            the line/aux jack, which is why LINE2 is the default here.

        config OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE2
            bool "LIN2 / RIN2 (line or aux jack on most boards)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE1
            bool "LIN1 / RIN1 (on-board microphone on most boards)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_DIFF
            bool "Differential"
            help
                Both pairs taken as one differential input. Only correct if
                the board is actually wired that way.
    endchoice

    choice OSYNTH_ES8388_OUTPUT
        prompt "ES8388 analogue output"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT_BOTH
        help
            Which output pairs to power. Driving one you are not using costs
            a little current and nothing else, so "both" is the safe answer
            when you are not sure how the board is wired.

        config OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT_BOTH
            bool "Both pairs (LOUT1/ROUT1 and LOUT2/ROUT2)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT1
            bool "LOUT1 / ROUT1 only (headphone jack on most boards)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT2
            bool "LOUT2 / ROUT2 only (speaker amp input on most boards)"
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_LOCAL_UI
        bool "Enable local UI (LCD + encoders + buttons)"
        default n
        help
            Local control surface for the synthesizer settings.
            Off by default; the component compiles to a stub.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_SERIAL_MIDI
        bool "Enable serial (UART/DIN) MIDI input"
        default n
        help
            Alternative MIDI input, mainly for the classic ESP32 which has
            no USB-OTG hardware.

    config OSYNTH_SERIAL_MIDI_RX_GPIO
        int "Serial MIDI RX GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_SERIAL_MIDI
        default 35 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 21 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 5
        help
            UART RX pin for DIN MIDI input (UART1, 31250 baud). Needs a
            proper MIDI input circuit (optocoupler, e.g. 6N138) — see
            docs/HARDWARE.md.

    config OSYNTH_LOOP_PERSIST
        bool "Persist looper tracks (save/load slots)"
        depends on SPIRAM
        default y
        help
            Adds loop.save / loop.load trigger params (0x0605/0x0606) that
            copy the S15 looper's track set between PSRAM and durable
            storage. Tracks are IMA-ADPCM (4:1 vs raw int16; mono sets,
            S19, halve that again) both live in PSRAM and in storage since
            S20, so saves and loads are straight copies. Storage backend
            and sizing below. Needs PSRAM, like the looper itself — so the
            S3 and P4, not the classic ESP32.

    choice OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE
        prompt "Looper track storage backend"
        depends on OSYNTH_LOOP_PERSIST
        default OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH

        config OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH
            bool "Internal flash (spare region, no extra hardware)"
            help
                Raw flash above the partition table (0x400000 up to the
                module's flash end — pick the module below). One slot.
                Flash writes stall XIP code fetches, so save/load require
                the looper transport to be stopped. (SPI_FLASH_AUTO_SUSPEND
                would soften the residual stalls, but leave it off: on
                unsupported chips it fails flash init and bootloops on
                IDF v6 — see the note in sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3.)

        config OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_SD
            bool "External SD card (SPI mode, FAT)"
            help
                SD card on the FSPI pins (configurable below), 8 slots,
                song-scale capacity, and no XIP interference — save/load
                work while the loop keeps playing. Wiring in
                docs/HARDWARE.md.
    endchoice

    choice OSYNTH_FLASH_MODEL
        prompt "Flash size on the module (sizes the loops region)"
        depends on OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH
        default OSYNTH_FLASH_8MB
        help
            The loops region runs from 0x400000 to the end of the chip, so
            this is the only thing that sets its size. Named after the flash
            size alone, not the module part number, because the same choice
            serves S3 modules (N8R8, N16R8) and P4 boards (N16R32) — the
            PSRAM half of those names is irrelevant here.

        config OSYNTH_FLASH_8MB
            bool "8 MB (4 MB loops region, ~87 s stereo ADPCM, 2x mono)"

        config OSYNTH_FLASH_16MB
            bool "16 MB (12 MB loops region, ~260 s stereo ADPCM, 2x mono)"
            help
                Also set Serial flasher config -> Flash size -> 16 MB so
                the image header matches the chip. The loops region is
                verified against the detected chip size at boot and
                trimmed (or disabled) with a warning if it doesn't fit.

        config OSYNTH_FLASH_32MB
            bool "32 MB (28 MB loops region, ~10 min stereo ADPCM, 2x mono)"
            help
                Same caveat as 16 MB: match Serial flasher config -> Flash
                size to the chip.
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_DRUM_SD_KITS
        bool "Load drum kits from an SD card"
        depends on SPIRAM
        default n
        help
            Lets the drum bus (S22) load kits from /sd/osynth/kits, either
            as `<name>.okit` images built by tools/gen_drumkit.py or as
            folders of .wav one-shots (name a file "03_snare.wav" to pin it
            to a slot). Kits are read into PSRAM, so this needs a PSRAM
            board; the factory kit embedded in the firmware works without
            it. Shares the SD bus and mount point with the looper's SD
            backend when both are enabled — whichever gets there first
            mounts the card.

    # The SD bus is shared: either feature turning it on makes the pins
    # relevant, so the pin options hang off this rather than off one of them.
    config OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        bool
        default y if OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_SD || OSYNTH_DRUM_SD_KITS

    config OSYNTH_SD_CS_GPIO
        int "SD card CS GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 39 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 10

    config OSYNTH_SD_SCK_GPIO
        int "SD card SCK GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 40 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 12

    config OSYNTH_SD_MOSI_GPIO
        int "SD card MOSI GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 41 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 11

    config OSYNTH_SD_MISO_GPIO
        int "SD card MISO GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 42 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 13

    config OSYNTH_RENDER_IN_IRAM
        bool "Place the audio render path in IRAM"
        default y
        help
            Marks the per-block render code (engines, voice manager, FX
            bus, looper, audio-task conversion) IRAM_ATTR so flash-cache
            misses from BLE/Wi-Fi or filesystem traffic cannot add jitter
            to the audio deadline. Costs roughly 20-30 KB of IRAM. Turn
            off if the image stops linking with "region iram0 overflowed".

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_MODULAR
        bool "Enable the modular patch graph engine (S28)"
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default n
        help
            Adds a fifth engine whose signal path is a node graph the app
            patches with cables, instead of a fixed chain. Costs an audio
            buffer pool in internal RAM (kMaxBufs x voices x block size x
            4 B — about 12 KB at the defaults) plus the render kernels in
            IRAM.

            Off by default on the classic ESP32: the buffer pool and the
            extra IRAM are affordable there, but a freely patched graph
            cannot be fused the way the four fixed engines are, and the
            headroom left after the FX bus does not reliably fit one. The
            fixed engines are unaffected either way.

    config OSYNTH_VOICES
        int "Polyphony (number of voices)"
        range 1 16
        default 8

    config OSYNTH_BLOCK_SIZE
        int "Audio block size (samples)"
        range 32 256
        default 64

    config OSYNTH_SAMPLE_RATE
        int "Sample rate (Hz)"
        default 48000
        help
            48000 matches the USB Audio (UAC2) streaming format.

    config OSYNTH_HEARTBEAT_MS
        int "Heartbeat log interval (ms, 0 = off)"
        range 0 600000
        default 5000
        help
            Period of the "alive | ..." status line. Set to 0 to silence
            it entirely.

            This is a diagnostic knob, not just cosmetics: the heartbeat
            is the only periodic event in the firmware slower than 500 ms,
            so if you hear a click at the same period as this log line,
            the logging path is the suspect and not the synthesis. Each
            line is a ~250-byte console write, and with the secondary
            USB-Serial-JTAG console enabled it also pokes a USB peripheral
            that shares the S3's USB PHY with the TinyUSB audio device.
            Change this to 30000 (or 0) and listen: if the clicks follow
            the new period, that is the cause; if they keep their old
            rhythm, look elsewhere.

endmenu
